\section{Two workflows}{

\strong{Self-contained (the default).} \code{tab_kable(css = TRUE)} and \code{tab_md(css = TRUE)} inline the
stylesheet with the table, so a single file works anywhere (RStudio/Positron Viewer, jamovi, a
standalone \code{.html}). Nothing to do.

\strong{Once per document.} In an \code{.Rmd}/\code{.qmd} with many tables, emit it once and let every table reuse
it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{```\{r, results = "asis"\}
options(tabxplor.kable_css = FALSE)
tab_css(theme = "auto")
```
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Every later \code{tab_kable()} then emits classes only. Two things to know: with \code{css = FALSE} and \strong{no}
\code{tab_css()} call the tables render uncoloured; and one stylesheet means one \code{theme} for the whole
document.
}

\section{Restyling a table}{

Nothing is written inline on a cell, so \strong{any} of the look can be overridden by adding your own
rules after the stylesheet -- no \code{!important} needed. Column widths in particular are left to the
browser (it sizes each column to its content); to pin one, style its role:
\preformatted{
.tabxplor-tab .tx-rv  { min-width: 10em; }   /* the row-variable levels column */
.tabxplor-tab .tx-tot { min-width: 5.5em; }  /* total columns                  */
.tabxplor-tab .tx-num { min-width: 4em; }    /* every number column            */
}
The roles a cell can carry: \code{.tx-l}/\code{.tx-r} (alignment), \code{.tx-num} (numbers), \code{.tx-rv} (the
row-variable levels column), \code{.tx-tot} (total columns), \code{.tx-bl}/\code{.tx-br} (side borders),
\code{.tx-b} (bold), \code{.tx-lbl}/\code{.tx-vname} (a variable name spanning its block), \code{.tx-pill} (a
background-coloured value), \code{.tx-span} (the variable-name header row), \code{.tx-foot} (the footnote).
Rows carry \code{.tx-bt}/\code{.tx-bb}/\code{.tx-bb2} (top / bottom / thick-bottom rules).
}

